// Practice quiz: how a request travels
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/http-and-javalin/how-a-request-travels

::o1 order of the trip::You press Enter on http\://library.example.org/books. Which order do the steps happen in? {
~Send the request, look up the host's IP address, connect, read the response#Not quite. A request travels over a connection, so the lookup and the connection come first.
~Connect to the host name, send the request, then look up the IP address for the response#Not quite. A connection is made to an IP address, so the name lookup happens before connecting.
~Look up the path, connect to the path, read the response#Not quite. The name lookup turns the host, not the path, into an IP address. The path is sent inside the request.
=Split the URL, look up the host's IP address, connect to that address and port, send the request, read the response#Correct. The browser needs the address from the name lookup before it can connect, and it needs the connection before it can send anything.
}

::o1 nobody listening::A browser opens http\://localhost\:9090/ but no program is listening on port 9090. What does the browser get? {
~A 404 Not Found response#Not quite. 404 is an answer from a server that received the request. Here nothing received it.
~A 500 Internal Server Error response#Not quite. 500 also comes from a running server. With nothing listening, no response arrives.
=A connection error, and no status code at all#Correct. The connection fails before any request is sent, so no server exists to answer with a status code.
~An empty 200 OK response#Not quite. A 200 needs a program that read the request and answered it.
}

::o2 missing port::Which port does the browser connect to for https\://news.example.com/today? {
~80#Not quite. 80 is the default for http. This URL uses https.
~8443#Not quite. 8443 is used only when the URL writes it after a colon.
~No port; https URLs do not use one#Not quite. Every connection uses a port. Leaving it out only means the default is used.
=443#Correct. When the port is left out, https means 443 and http means 80.
}

::o2 query part::In http\://localhost\:7070/authors?country\=pe&limit\=5, what is the query? {
~/authors#Not quite. That is the path, the resource being asked for.
=country\=pe&limit\=5#Correct. The query is everything after the question mark, as name and value pairs joined by an ampersand.
~7070#Not quite. That is the port, the program on the computer.
~localhost\:7070/authors#Not quite. That is the host, port and path together, not the options after the question mark.
}

::o3 meaning of localhost::Your friend opens http\://localhost\:7070/ on their own laptop while your server runs on yours. What happens? {
=Their browser looks for a server on their own laptop, not on yours#Correct. localhost always means the computer you are using, so the request never leaves their machine.
~Their browser reaches your server, because localhost names the server's computer#Not quite. localhost is not a name for any one server. It always means the computer making the request.
~Their browser reaches whichever laptop on the network started a server first#Not quite. A localhost request never leaves the machine, so no other laptop is involved.
~The request goes to a public internet server named localhost#Not quite. localhost is a reserved name that resolves to the loopback address on the same computer.
}

::o3 what a port picks::On one computer, a Java server listens on port 7070 and a database listens on port 5432. What does the port number in a URL decide? {
=Which listening program on that computer receives the connection#Correct. The host picks the computer and the port picks one program listening on it.
~Which computer on the network receives the connection#Not quite. That is the host's job. The port works inside the chosen computer.
~Which file on the server is returned#Not quite. The path names the resource. The port only picks the program.
~How fast the response is sent#Not quite. A port is a number that selects a program, not a speed setting.
}
